      "side": 1,
      "message": "I still think this is a bad practice. This is equivalent to:\n```\nstruct Digest([u8; DIGEST_SIZE]);\nstruct VerifiedData {\n  kernel_digest: Digest,\n  initrd_digest: Digest,\n}\n```\nbut instead of accessing the individual fields, you use offsets everywhere, ie. `digests[..DIGEST_SIZE]` and `digests[DIGEST_SIZE..]`. It will get even more confusing once there is a third or fourth entry. \n\nI get that you\u0027re doing this because you can then hash the whole array in one go, but you could instead update your \"digester\" twice:\n```\nlet mut digester \u003d sha::Sha256::new();\ndigester.update(verified_data.kernel_digest);\ndigester.update(verified_data.initrd_digest);\ndigester.finish()\n```\nThis has the same semantics and does not allocate any more memory. Why not do that?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 31,
        "startChar": 17,
        "endLine": 31,
        "endChar": 39
      },
      "revId": "78d6a6707e7957c106248166ea45e0c746223148",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "dbf5e287_760a112a",
